so I've been using and loving the  Microsoft band too for a while now but  despite all of its great qualities which  I talked about in the review Microsoft  does seem to have some quality control  issues I had to return the device about  five times it broke again and I thought  that's it I'm gonna try something new so  I bought myself the garmin vivoactive HR  and i must say it's rather splendid  when I first got my hands on the  vivoactive HR is actually a little bit  disappointed it feels a little flimsy  and plasticky in the hands and the  screen is very dim certainly a step down  from the Microsoft band 2 and also not  half as clear as the promotional  materials made it look on the plus side  the full-color display is a bit fancier  than the Fitbit surge at least and it's  nice that you can download custom watch  faces - it's comfy enough as well though  little on a large side to be truly  practical but a fitness tracker  shouldn't be a fashion statement anyways  and this device has got it where it  counts the starters it comes packed with  all the sensors you might need including  the heart rate monitor altimeter compass  GPS pedometer etc and these all allow  you to track everything you'd expect  such as your daily activity your sleep  quality and all manner of activities  like running swimming cycling  weightlifting skiing walking and more  while many of these features are fairly  standard on fitness trackers these days  oh I like about the vivoactive hey  Chou's that nothing's left out there no  compromises for example many devices  like the Microsoft band 2 and Fitbit  surge aren't waterproof the vivoactive  HR is safe up to 5 meters meaning you  can swim with it other devices leave out  the GPS making them poor choices as  running watches or they are very good  for weight lifting the gear fit - for  example doesn't let you use the  heartrate monitor during workouts the  vivoactive hey charlie other hand  doesn't make those kind of compromises  and another example of this is the  battery life which can last up to 7 days  on a single charge even when using the  GPS and other things regularly and that  GPS actually gets an upgrade as well  seeing as the beaver active HR is  capable of using GLONASS to connect more  quickly and get more accurate location  data this is Garmin better known for  their Sat Navs after all all this is  made possible thanks to a great heart  rate sensor while no wrist worn heart  rate sensor is going to be perfect this  one does take a lot of readings and  seems to be at the front of the pack for  accuracy there is also the option to  pair it with a more accurate Garmin  chess strap if you want to more  importantly it uses a smart update  frequency which is something you don't  hear that much about this means it won't  just check your heart rate once every 10  minutes regardless of what you're doing  throughout the day and that allows it to  give more accurate information regarding  your calories burned it doesn't miss any  quick runs to the shop for example or  sexy pillow fights for those who like  tracking calories or macros this makes  big difference and gives a much more  reliable calculation the garment a  percolates all this data in a very  comprehensive manner to giving you  screens and screens to read through when  you're done training I particularly  enjoyed seeing my top speed stride  length and cadence after a run that said  a few things are noticeably absent such  as a score for overall sleep quality  which is implicit not explicit or VA to  max on the other hand is particularly  effective at motivating you to moved  that's thanks to a step target that  adjusts the better suit your lifestyle  and a move bar that fills up the longer  you remain inactive the streak of days  where you meet your calorie goal is also  clever the OCD in you will want to work  extra hard not to break that streak but  where all that data falls down isn't  just how obtuse it is seriously the  Garmin Connect app looks like it was  designed by someone on hard drugs  nothing is we expect it to be there are  two separate ways to look at your sleep  which showed different information  calories burned are surprisingly hard to  find both for each day and after workout  it's not a problem if you're willing to  go digging but it's hardly intuitive and  it's a bit of a pain when even months  later you're still struggling to  remember where you need to go to find  certain information also disappointing  is how clearly aimed at runners the  devices while it does support weight  lifting insists on telling you how much  distance you've covered as the main  metric when you finish the workout and  of course that figure is normally a fat  zero which isn't exactly motivating it  provides just as much information in  this regard as the competition but it  still feels like a bit of an  afterthought rather than one of the  intended purposes what's great about the  software though is the SmartWatch stuff  this is a fitness tracker first in a  SmartWatch second but the ability to get  notifications on your wrist is something  I've grown used to with the Microsoft  fans and it's nice to find it present  and correct here better still have a  number of different apps available to  install which can greatly expand the  watch as capabilities these include  music controls a stargazing app maps a  calculator that missing vo to metric and  even flappy birds it's an absolutely  rubbish version of flappy birds sure but  it's definitely neat for showing what  the device is capable of overall then  this is by no means a perfect device and  the UI screen brightness and build  quality could all do with some work but  as a reliable way to track all kinds of  different fitness data for days on end  without a charge it's quite possibly  the best option out there
 

